Direct presentation without modifying VcXsrv
=============================================

Purpose
-------

Replace VirtualGL's finished-image path with direct presentation of the GL
texture virglrenderer has already created on the Windows GPU. Applications must
open ordinary GLX/EGL contexts through Mesa: no vglrun wrapper, LD_PRELOAD,
faker library, application patch, frame readback, or XPutImage presentation.

VcXsrv 1.14.2.1 is immutable. Its build is an installer input that cannot be
reconstructed as part of the normal MoCoLinux build, so no rung may depend on a
patched X server. VcXsrv remains responsible for the things it already does
well: X protocol semantics, outer HWND lifetime, input, focus, stacking,
clipboard, titles, icons, menus, shapes, and ordinary CPU-rendered X drawing.

The split is therefore a control plane and a render plane:

    X client ---------------------------------------> stock VcXsrv
       |                                               outer HWND
       | Mesa GLX/EGL                                    ^
       v                                                 |
    virtio-gpu -> cogpu -> virglrenderer texture -> WGL child HWND

Only commands, resource identity, damage, and fences cross the cooperative
boundary. Finished pixels do not.

The unmodified-server seam
--------------------------

The shipped VcXsrv gives an external process all that the presenter needs:

  * every multiwindow HWND carries its XID in the Win32 property
    "vcxsrv_wid_prop_rl";
  * its client window has WS_CLIPCHILDREN, so VcXsrv's GDI repaint is clipped
    around a child owned by cogpu;
  * VcXsrv's own native-GL implementation uses a WS_CHILD | WS_DISABLED GL
    window. A disabled child does not steal the input which belongs to the
    outer X window.

Cogpu can therefore enumerate the stock server's windows, match an XID, create
and size its own WGL child, and destroy it without injecting code into VcXsrv
or drawing into a device context whose pixel format somebody else owns.

Non-negotiable invariants
-------------------------

  * The installed VcXsrv files remain byte-for-byte stock.
  * An accelerated application starts normally. No per-application launcher or
    preload environment is part of correctness.
  * A presented frame is never read back to guest or host system memory.
  * The host samples the virglrenderer resource in cogpu's existing WGL share
    group. A GL texture name never crosses a process boundary.
  * Every buffer has explicit acquire and release ordering. Resource IDs alone
    are not lifetime protection.
  * Failure removes the WGL child and exposes VcXsrv's ordinary drawable; it
    must not leave a permanent black overlay.
  * OpenGL/EGL delivery and Vulkan delivery are separate projects. Virgl does
    not become a Vulkan implementation by acquiring a presentation path.

What applications must see
--------------------------

Direct presentation is not complete merely because pixels move quickly. The
guest graphics stack must look like an ordinary direct-rendering Linux stack
to applications which make security and feature decisions from it:

  * /dev/dri/renderD128 is the device Mesa opens and virgl is the renderer;
  * glXIsDirect and glxinfo report a direct context, never indirect GLX;
  * ordinary GLX applications consume Mesa's normal DRI3 provider, including
    its DRI images, buffer queue, buffer-age tracking and native fences. A
    guest-side transport may terminate the final Linux dma-buf/Present handoff
    in CoPresent because an fd cannot be passed into a Windows X server, but it
    must not replace DRI3 with indirect GL, an Xlib-only renderer or a wrapper;
  * a program which directly queries the X server's DRI3 or Present extension
    still needs a compatible query facade. Stock VcXsrv cannot advertise those
    Linux fd protocols, so that server-query case must not be confused with the
    now-working normal GLX DRI3 loader;
  * no vglrun, libvglfaker, LD_PRELOAD, application injection, or executable
    patch is present in the launch command or process mappings.

Steam, Firefox and sandboxed clients are acceptance tests for this contract,
not special cases to spoof. R1 proves only the host texture/display seam; R2
and R3 make the contract real for ordinary clients.

Rungs
-----

R0 -- prove the immutable VcXsrv/Win32 seam

    Add an opt-in cogpu host probe. It locates a visible VcXsrv HWND through
    "vcxsrv_wid_prop_rl", creates a disabled WGL child with its own pixel
    format and a context sharing with cogpu's base context, and renders a sharp
    diagnostic animation with several independently moving shapes in that
    child. It then removes the child and invalidates the parent.

    PASS: the animation remains inside the chosen X window; the outer window
    can be moved, resized, focused and typed into; another window clips it; and
    the original X contents repaint when the probe exits. VcXsrv is unmodified.

R1 -- present an actual virglrenderer resource

    Wrap virgl_renderer_resource_get_info(), retain a presentation reference
    to the resource, and draw its host tex_id in the R0 child with a minimal
    shader. Handle orientation and the formats Mesa actually produces. Restore
    virglrenderer's context after every present.

    PASS: a known guest-rendered test texture appears in the child with no
    glReadPixels, StretchDIBits, XPutImage, or frame-sized host/guest message.
    Destroying the guest resource while it is bound is refused or deferred.

    HOST-SIDE PASS (2026-08-09): an in-guest `moco-gl glxgears` created a
    300x300 virgl colour target. Cogpu decoded Mesa's in-stream TRANSFER3D,
    matched its resource to VcXsrv XID 0x200002, obtained host texture 2 from
    virglrenderer, and sampled it in the shared WGL child. The visible gears
    ran near 60 Hz; the log measured 1,302 direct frames and 57.1 fps. On
    process exit the guest unreferenced resource 3, cogpu removed the child and
    released its GL binding before virglrenderer deletion, and the daemon
    remained alive.

    This test still launched through VirtualGL solely to produce a known frame
    and its existing readback command supplied the temporary selection point.
    The presenter itself sampled the host texture and performed no readback,
    but the fallback path still copied the frame in parallel. Therefore this
    was not the no-VGL application-path gate; R2 now replaces that temporary
    selection point with explicit fenced presentation.

R2 -- make presentation a fenced cooperative service

    Define a versioned CoPresent protocol and a guest broker. The broker owns a
    Unix socket and accepts a drawable XID, dma-buf, dimensions, damage and an
    acquire fence from an unprivileged client. It PRIME-imports the dma-buf on
    the virtio render node, asks DRM_VIRTGPU_RESOURCE_INFO for the imported
    handle's host resource id, and sends fixed-size BIND, PRESENT and UNBIND
    records over a small pinned shared-memory ring. The host returns RELEASE
    records. Connection and resource generations make stale messages harmless
    after either side restarts.

    IMPLEMENTED (2026-08-09): `moco-present-broker` accepts dma-buf and native
    fence FDs over a SOCK_SEQPACKET socket, PRIME-imports each buffer on
    renderD128, and publishes only fixed 64-byte records through an 8 KiB
    pinned region. Cogpu waits the acquire fence, retains the virgl resource,
    samples its existing host texture, returns RELEASE, and tears down the WGL
    child on UNBIND or generation reset. Neither ring can contain pixels.

    HARDWARE RESULT: the standalone two-buffer producer submitted and received
    RELEASE for all 116,587 frames in a 126-second interactive run. It rebound
    cleanly at 1000x700, 1200x760, 800x500 and 1500x800; the host log records
    matched UNBINDs for both resources at every size and after the X window's
    normal close event. An unmodified GLX client using the same service later
    completed 33,027 releases in 75 seconds at 900x600, settled at 446.3
    no-copy fps, and cleanly UNBINDed. The uninterrupted ten-minute producer
    soak remains the final acceptance run, not a missing protocol component.

    PASS: a standalone guest program cycles two buffers for at least ten
    minutes while windows are resized and destroyed. No buffer is sampled
    before acquire or reused before release, and daemon restart leaves no child
    windows behind.

R3 -- normal 64-bit GLX applications

    Extend Mesa's existing DRI3 GLX loader instead of adding another Xlib GLX
    implementation. It opens the ordinary virtio-gpu render node, loads
    Gallium virgl, creates normal X windows for control/events, and preserves
    Mesa's DRI images, buffer queue, buffer age and native fences. Only the
    operation the Linux X server would normally perform at the end is replaced:
    CoPresent receives the dma-buf and fence because Windows VcXsrv cannot.
    Selection is system-wide from the MoCo device and broker, not a wrapper
    around an application.

    PASS for this rung: unmodified 64-bit GLX programs enter Mesa's DRI3
    provider, report direct virgl, render in VcXsrv windows, and contain neither
    VirtualGL nor a preload faker in /proc/PID/maps. Resize, drawable lifetime
    and broker loss must fail cleanly. Programs which directly query the X
    server's DRI3 extension, EGL, Vulkan and 32-bit clients belong to their
    explicit later coverage rungs.

    75% CHECKPOINT (2026-08-09): the canonical Mesa build now uses `-Dglx=dri`
    and produces a matched `libGL.so.1.2.0` and `libgallium-26.1.6.so`.
    Unmodified `glxinfo -B` logs "Using DRI3 with MoCo CoPresent", reports
    `direct rendering: Yes`, virgl on the GeForce GT 730, and OpenGL 4.2.
    VcXsrv 1.14's fatal GLXBadDrawable response to Mesa's optional raw-window
    drawable-type query is avoided only in MoCo mode; ordinary X window
    attributes provide the same information without changing VcXsrv.

    Unmodified `glxgears` presented 1,343 and 1,412 frames in consecutive
    five-second samples (268.6 and 282.2 fps). Its live process map contained
    the matched CoPresent libGL and Gallium libraries and no libvgl. A heavier
    `glxspheres64` run at 1920x1080 with 3,513,600 polygons per frame sustained
    about 15.5 fps while Windows measured 81--87% utilization for
    cogpu-daemon's 3D engine. That proves genuine GPU execution but also exposes
    the next bottleneck: the correctness-first presenter drains and releases
    every frame synchronously. An emergency XPutImage fallback exists for a
    lost broker; deliberate stop/restart and longer soak remain acceptance
    tests, not assumptions.

R4 -- EGL, browsers and the Steam client

    Reuse the same Mesa winsys from EGL/X11, including explicit fences,
    buffer-age and damage semantics required by Chromium-derived clients.
    Exercise nested X drawables by resolving their top-level XID and offset
    before positioning the WGL child.

    PASS: Firefox and Steam start without moco-gl, their GPU processes use
    /dev/dri/renderD128, and rendered content survives resize, minimize,
    occlusion, menus and repeated open/close cycles.

R5 -- 32-bit and Steam Runtime integration

    Build and package the same Mesa/broker client ABI for i686. Install both
    graphics stacks where Steam's pressure-vessel runtime imports host GPU
    drivers; do not rely on the runtime accidentally inheriting a development
    path.

    PASS: both 64-bit and 32-bit glxinfo select virgl, Steam's system report
    sees the same renderer in both ABIs, and a 32-bit OpenGL title presents
    without VirtualGL.

R6 -- production window semantics and recovery

    Complete drawable-to-top-level resolution, offsets, multiple GL drawables
    per outer window, shaped windows, DPI, occlusion throttling, buffer release
    on minimize, X server restart, guest restart and host display reset. Keep
    ordinary X-rendered popups and decorations outside the GL child.

    PASS: desktop and application soak across XP x64, Windows 7 and Windows 10
    shows no orphan child, black repaint, input loss or resource leak.

R7 -- performance gate and removal of VirtualGL from the default path

    Instrument command bytes, present records, fence latency, SwapBuffers time,
    missed releases and host/guest CPU. Make direct presentation the default
    only after the fallback remains usable and the measured path carries no
    frame-sized traffic.

    PASS: steady-state present traffic is metadata-sized, frame rate is paced
    by the application/vsync rather than readback, and the normal launch path
    contains neither vglrun nor libvglfaker. Retain VirtualGL only as an
    explicitly selected compatibility fallback until a later release removes
    it.

    NEXT AFTER THE R3 CHECKPOINT: pull the performance part of this rung forward.
    The current call chain is render, acquire-fence wait, host presentation draw,
    glFinish, SwapBuffers, RELEASE, then the next frame. Replace glFinish with a
    host GL fence, retire RELEASE asynchronously per buffer, and permit Mesa's
    queue to keep two or three frames in flight. Record time separately for the
    guest fence, virglrenderer, the sampling fence, SwapBuffers and the metadata
    round trip. Installer integration remains last so it packages the optimized
    path rather than freezing the checkpoint implementation into the image.

R8 -- Vulkan/Proton, separately

    Add virtio-gpu Venus and a Vulkan WSI presentation path using the same HWND
    and CoPresent lifetime machinery. Do not route Vulkan through the OpenGL
    texture API or claim Proton/DXVK support before this rung passes.

    PASS: vulkaninfo reports a hardware Venus device in both ABIs and a DXVK
    test presents through CoPresent with explicit Vulkan fences.

Current implementation boundary
-------------------------------

R0, R1 and the R2 cooperative service are implemented. The service adds a
guest broker and matched host protocol but does not modify the driver, guest
kernel or VcXsrv. `--present-r2` remains deliberately opt-in while normal
release shortcuts retain VirtualGL.

R3 has reached the normal 64-bit GLX DRI3 loader and is approximately 75% of
the way to a usable replacement for that release path. A complete repository
patch against the checksum-pinned Mesa 26.1.6 release builds the matched DRI
`libGL` and Gallium libraries, and the reversible system selector has been
validated in the running guest. This is not yet the rootfs or installer
default. The immediate work is asynchronous multi-buffered release and
performance measurement; packaging is deliberately last. Direct server DRI3
queries, EGL/browsers, 32-bit clients and Vulkan remain separately named rungs.
Build, selection, rollback and accepted hashes are in
`doc/building-copresent`.
